  "worked_solution": "First, we reduce our congruence to a congruence of the form $ax≡b,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+4 & ≡23\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 5x+4-4 & ≡23-4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 5x & ≡19\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 5x & ≡1\\cdot 13+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 5x & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=5$ and $n=13$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(5,13)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$ To find the inverse of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$, we compute the exponents of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2} & ≡25≡1\\cdot 13+12≡12 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {5}^{3} & ≡12\\cdot 5≡60≡4\\cdot 13+8≡8 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ {5}^{4} & ≡8\\cdot 5≡40≡3\\cdot 13+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $8\\cdot 5≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13),$ which means that $8$ is the multiplicative inverse of $5$ (modulo $13$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $8,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & ≡6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 8\\cdot 5x & ≡8\\cdot 6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ (8\\cdot 5)x & ≡48 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡9+3\\cdot 13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13) \\\\ x & ≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13).$",
